      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Automotive publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on whether you can actually drive and write, or just write.</p>

            <p>The buff books and buying platforms do not take guest posts. The enthusiast sites take contributed reviews and analysis. The EV and classic-car outlets want a specialist angle. The regional titles are more open. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Buff books &amp; buying platforms</td><td>Car and Driver, MotorTrend, Autocar, Cars.com, Edmunds, Autotrader</td><td>Not guest posts. Exclusive data, a first drive, or expert commentary from a credentialed journalist</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Enthusiast &amp; review sites</td><td>Jalopnik, CarThrottle, Carscoops, PistonHeads, CarBuzz, The Truth About Cars</td><td>Contributed reviews, data-led pieces, reported analysis with photos</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>EV &amp; classic-car specialists</td><td>InsideEVs, Electrek, Hagerty, Bring a Trailer, Hemmings, Petrolicious, Silodrome</td><td>A specialist angle: ownership data, restoration, market analysis, technology deep dives</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Regional &amp; trade titles</td><td>CarsGuide, Drive, GoAuto, Wheels.ca, Car Dealer Magazine, Motor Trader</td><td>Local market angles, buying guides, trade and dealer analysis</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Spam Score</td><td>Moz's 0 to 4 estimate of how many spam signals a site shows</td><td>Treat 3 as a reason to look closely before you pitch</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> the automotive affiliate space is crowded with thin "10 best SUVs" content that all reads the same. A placement on an enthusiast site with real driving impressions, or a data piece on a buff book, is worth more than a dozen of those.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 automotive sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating, estimated monthly traffic and Spam Score. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; dead, parked and merged sites were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th><th>Spam</th></tr>
                </thead>
