                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                      <td>{s.spam}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for automotive editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the drive or the data.</strong> "I ran this EV as my only car for 3 months in winter" or "we pulled 10 years of depreciation data on hot hatches" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Prove you can do the job.</strong> Published clips, your own photography, a press-fleet history, or a genuine specialist niche.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your EV coverage" or "for the classic-car desk" shows you read the publication.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> Your own images, a data set, a spec table, a video.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> Buff books want a tight news piece or first drive. Enthusiast sites want a structured review. Regional titles want a local buying angle.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any dealer, brand or PR relationship</strong> up front.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had no car or part to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it is a spec sheet for your inventory, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic "best family SUVs 2026" listicle. Every site has a rolling one.</li>
              <li>Submit a review written from the press release without driving the car.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch to a buff book and a regional dealer title.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("used cars near me") at your dealership.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated filler with no driving impressions. In automotive it reads as generic within a paragraph.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on enthusiast and buff-book sites whose readers actually buy cars will do more for rankings and sales than a long list of thin car-blog links. Use the Spam Score and traffic columns to find your few.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
